On 2005-01-13, Boudewijn Waijers <kroisos@REMOVETHISWORD.home.nl> wrote:
> I would abolish magic mapping. [...]

> I would abolish identify. It is way too powerful. [...]

> I would abolish taming. As a spell, it's too powerful. [...]

... and so on.

I wholeheartedly dissent.  I don't think making the game harder is a
worthwhile goal, it's plenty hard already.  In changing the game, I
think we want to look at ways of making it more fun, more varied, more
interesting.  I wouldn't want any of the currently existing spells
abolished, but I do feel that there are too few spells, and inventing
more of them would make the game more interesting.  It would also lead
to people thinking up more and weirder ways to play.

> I would abolish create monster. The spell is currently used more as a
> "create offer fodder". Alternatively, make summoned creatures no longer
> carry items or be eligable for offering, or both.

I don't see anything wrong with "create offer fodder", but I can see
why some people think of it as cheating.  As with other things that
some people see as cheating but which make the game more varied and
interesting for others, it could be a conduct:
  "never sacrificed any monsters you deliberately summoned."

It shouldn't be hard for the game to keep track of that.  If there
isn't already a bit in struct mon for "summoned by player", it's
pretty easy to add.  I'd recommend exceptions for:
 - random "summoning" such as by drinking at a fountain
 - the first use of an unidentified wand/scroll/bag


... and now, what other interesting, perhaps even humorous,
but potentially useful, spells could be added?  :-)

